Dyspnoea
Palliative & End of Life
Distressing air hunger, Accessory muscle use, Speaking strictly in single words

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-024
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of breathlessness at rest causing extreme panic and distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient was experiencing an unremitting, severe subjective sensation of suffocating and air hunger even at absolute rest, accompanied by extreme panic and an inability to speak in full sentences. The severe respiratory work and distress required an urgent after-hours medical assessment to rule out acute reversible causes, provide immediate parenteral symptom relief, and break the cycle of panic and hyperventilation prior to regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Patient gasps: "Can't... get... enough... air. Going... to... die."
History of Present Illness: Over the last 48 hours, the patient has developed a subjective, severe, and highly distressing sensation of suffocating, breathlessness, and distinct air hunger. There is an unremitting, severe tachypnea strictly present continuously even at absolute rest. The spouse notes exacerbation of breathlessness explicitly triggered by completely minimal, trivial exertion, such as turning over in bed. There is severe anxiety, extreme panic, and an overwhelming subjective fear of impending suffocation. The patient is exhibiting an inability to speak strictly in full, complete sentences entirely without explicitly pausing for breath. Tachycardia and profound diaphoresis directly secondary strictly to explicitly severely increased respiratory work are evident.
Functional Status: Strictly bedbound due to severe respiratory distress upon any movement.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound caregiver distress watching the patient struggle; patient is physically exhausted from the continuous work of breathing.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/95 mmHg | HR: 125 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 32 bpm | SpO2: 88% on room air | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient is sitting bolt upright, leaning forward, diaphoretic, wide-eyed with terror, and gasping for air.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals obligate use of accessory respiratory muscles specifically including the sternocleidomastoid and intercostals. Nasal flaring is prominently explicitly visible strictly during normal tidal inspiration. Central cyanosis is distinctly visualizing precisely around the perioral area strictly in severe end-stage hypoxia. Auscultation reveals globally diminished air entry but no distinct wheeze or crackles. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the observed distressing air hunger, obvious accessory muscle use, and the patient speaking strictly in single words.
Environmental Observations: The room is stuffy; windows are closed. No supplemental oxygen is currently present in the home.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has been using a salbutamol inhaler repeatedly with zero relief, as the dyspnea is driven by advanced lung volume loss rather than acute bronchospasm.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Dyspnoea - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Panic/Breathlessness Cycle)
Differentials:

Acute pulmonary embolism - Ruled out/unlikely given the insidious 48-hour build-up, absence of sudden pleuritic chest pain, and no hemoptysis or unilateral calf swelling.

Acute pulmonary edema (Heart Failure) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of basal crackles, lack of elevated JVP, and no peripheral edema.

Severe bronchospasm (Asthma/COPD exacerbation) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absolute lack of wheeze on auscultation and complete failure to respond to high-dose bronchodilators.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a severe, acute crisis of end-stage palliative dyspnea compounded by extreme secondary panic. The objective findings of profound tachypnea, significant accessory muscle use, and hypoxia (SpO2 88%) confirm critical respiratory failure. Because the patient is entirely exhausted from the massive work of breathing and is locked in a terrifying cycle of hyperventilation and anxiety, delaying assessment until standard hours would result in catastrophic respiratory muscle fatigue, prolonged psychological torture, and inevitable terminal decompensation. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely critical to administer rapid-acting parenteral opioids and sedatives to slow the respiratory rate, blunt the air hunger, and restore comfort.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat subcutaneous morphine (to explicitly reduce the central perception of breathlessness) and subcutaneous midazolam (to break the acute panic/anxiety cycle). Directed a fan to blow cool air continuously across the patient's face (stimulating the trigeminal nerve to reduce air hunger).
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the terrifying sensation of not getting enough air (trigger), explicitly focus your eyes directly on your spouse's face and match their slow, deep breathing rhythm (behavior). This grounds you, preventing the panic from taking over and causing you to hyperventilate, which actively saves your energy and reduces the suffocating feeling (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the community palliative care team to establish a continuous subcutaneous infusion of morphine/midazolam tomorrow morning.
DME/Safety (Optional): Urgent arrangement for home oxygen therapy purely for symptomatic comfort.
Follow-up: Palliative care nursing review in 12 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient becomes completely unarousable, exhibits a respiratory rate below 8 breaths per minute, or if the severe gasping and panic return despite the subcutaneous medications, the spouse must contact the 24/7 palliative care triage line or 000 immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The medications will not reverse the underlying lung damage; they act directly on the brain to stop the sensation of suffocation and reduce the panic.
Clinical Course: The breathlessness will be a continuous symptom requiring strict, scheduled daily medications to keep the patient comfortable.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Spouse: Ensure the fan remains on and windows are open. Administer the prescribed breakthrough under-the-tongue medications at the very first sign of the panic returning. Do not wait for a full crisis.

